{"agent_id":"pp3g-4gKW-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T01:07:24.320228657Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"1acb2141-10eb-41ff-9005-5e15c6ae7525","comment_uuid":"cbac7d8d-f663-4bde-aa9e-e1cbf6dc179f","author":"pp3g-4gKW-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","content":"[action] Discovery: session work initially encountered a stale-lock warning; lock claim/steal path resolved it and issue #123 is now active.","created_at":"2026-08-03T01:07:24.314204673Z","kind":"note","signed_by":"SHA256:sHKRvBTE+ocai2GCkpU1ZGtRB3kQ0UxaT9Cky2VdpfA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgxfVGmOc8jDUcExqYhmcSMaTgp00ckBXqWN8orhUnXdgAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABANUBIj3GD+vD4eDr/GuMUeDoupE5ORS3VAdBWFbv7TVovYyI9S/LWZZ/0UyvUoNLsKKy7htnnAD+VOQ8H8HY8CA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:sHKRvBTE+ocai2GCkpU1ZGtRB3kQ0UxaT9Cky2VdpfA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgxfVGmOc8jDUcExqYhmcSMaTgp00ckBXqWN8orhUnXdgAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQDB05+Uw4YWERthbRQHqcyRKSq6gL6L/YYhFQinuNaCkonoHSUjGdHGwhryjg/oABimOLsCe5M55HR9x60xbPgc="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-4gKW-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","agent_seq":6,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T01:07:39.456741900Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"1acb2141-10eb-41ff-9005-5e15c6ae7525","comment_uuid":"3060ce4f-4fb1-4d94-a8bb-57e98fe6bc07","author":"pp3g-4gKW-adversarial-review-of-the-three-candidate-progress","content":"Recommendation: adopt a combination of (1) MANDATORY CHECKPOINT COMMENTS as the durable operator-facing record and (3) KICKOFF.md HEARTBEAT/CHECK-IN as the enforcement mechanism. Make the template check-in emit a structured --kind observation comment and run crosslink sync; use (2) SESSION-ACTION BREADCRUMBS only as supplementary low-noise telemetry, not the sole feedback channel. No mechanism makes the operator see updates literally without polling/notifications, so the contract must define the operator-facing view and sync boundary explicitly.\n\nOption 1 — MANDATORY CHECKPOINT COMMENTS\nMUST FIX: Define exact milestones (start/plan, meaningful midpoint or blocker, verification/finish), required fields (state, completed, next, blocker), and require sync after posting or at a bounded interval. Evidence: the #120/#121 pattern showed worktree comments do not reach the hub until sync; crosslink-fork.md also warns stale rehydration can drop local-only comments. Without this, comments are durable only locally and cannot distinguish silent work from a dead agent at the hub.\nSHOULD CONSIDER: Keep comments milestone-based rather than time-noisy; require an immediate blocker comment and a final result. This is the strongest visibility and failure-detection option once synced: a missing expected checkpoint is actionable, though it still requires the operator/status consumer to inspect it.\nNIT: Standardize a short prefix/status vocabulary to make issue timelines scannable.\n\nOption 2 — SESSION-ACTION BREADCRUMBS\nMUST FIX: Specify who consumes actions and how they are synchronized/ surfaced. The playbook currently says to record breadcrumbs and the guide documents the command, but neither guarantees operator visibility; session actions are not a substitute for an issue comment or explicit hub sync.\nSHOULD CONSIDER: Retain them for high-frequency internal breadcrumbs and process continuity, with timestamps/agent identity; use them to support working-vs-stalled diagnosis only when the operator can inspect current session state and sync is known-good. They are lower implementation cost than issue-comment policy but weaker as an operator contract.\nNIT: Avoid posting every tool call; milestone or 5–10 minute cadence is enough.\n\nOption 3 — KICKOFF.md HEARTBEAT/CHECK-IN\nMUST FIX: A template sentence is not a scheduler. Define concrete check-in points/cadence, the exact command(s), mandatory sync, and escalation when a check-in is missed. The current custom-template guidance lists setup/plan/implement/sync/final steps but no progress heartbeat, while the playbook warns RUNNING flags can persist on dead processes.\nSHOULD CONSIDER: Use it to enforce Option 1 at start, midpoint, blocker, and verification; make cadence task-aware so a short task is not burdened with excessive comments. This has moderate docs/prompt cost and the best compliance leverage, but by itself has poor visibility/durability because it produces no transport or consumer.\nNIT: Keep the check-in block small and copy-pasteable; do not require a separate reporting format per agent type.\n\nTimeout guidance: The evidence indicates timeout length is a primary problem, not merely a feedback-mechanism problem: #120 completed a four-file fix in roughly 2–3 minutes on a 40m timeout, and the issue history reports review/doc/port tasks at <=30m. Set task-matched ceilings with modest setup/verification headroom: trivial <=10m, simple documentation/review ~15–20m, multi-file/port ~30m; reserve 45m/1h+ for genuinely larger or externally blocked work. A shorter timeout limits blind waiting; checkpoints then provide early evidence of progress or stalling. Do not treat a heartbeat as justification for retaining a blanket 1h default.","created_at":"2026-08-03T01:07:39.450392756Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:sHKRvBTE+ocai2GCkpU1ZGtRB3kQ0UxaT9Cky2VdpfA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgxfVGmOc8jDUcExqYhmcSMaTgp00ckBXqWN8orhUnXdgAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAXfFwory/ci9k+ACOcRlbtcB4ooyNri41PtNgZbmFdtXQYzJ6koU/Uh/O5uQCBJG42d9sUDXQOaKlV8A7UcTaAw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:sHKRvBTE+ocai2GCkpU1ZGtRB3kQ0UxaT9Cky2VdpfA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgxfVGmOc8jDUcExqYhmcSMaTgp00ckBXqWN8orhUnXdgAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQGxYxxwwLqOJJuugSQKMyTUWalrJCxF0u3zsyj3dezbEfupdY46kuiStgHMgWuRAcjYWXaLtKIqyBUNlwZil/QQ="}
